<characters>
  <character>
    <name>Cleo</name>
    <metatype>Human</metatype>
    <background>It’s Cleo, and I know what you’re thinking. Cliche'  right? "red pill" or the "blue pill", a choice between seeing the world between worlds, or staying fat, dumb and happy as you are. What’s in it? Red Pill’s a chance to come back alive, no matter what, and the blue pill keeps you as your mamma, or whatever flea dirt ripper doc made you. It’s Cleo, and I know what you’re thinking. Cliche'  right? "red pill" or the "blue pill", a choice between seeing the world between worlds, or staying fat, dumb and happy as you are. What’s in it? Red Pill’s a chance to come back alive, no matter what, and the blue pill keeps you as your mamma, or whatever flea dirt ripper doc made you. Here are the rewinder reasons. 1\) It's a ride. Sure the scenery changes with the weather, but the ride is always the same: you get up and go to work. In red pill you accept that life is going to be tedious if you're lazy. What’s not to like about that? 2\) It shows how the world really is. The blue pill is a lie. Real life is brutal, no matter what, except for the leechers that lead you around by the nose. Or those who claim that real life is filled with opulence and wealth and all the things people desire. That's a lie too, a sellout to the fat, dumb and happy world. Red pill says "shit. The world is just like you’d expect and it’s shit. A lot of fat, dumb and happy people know it, too. But there are many, many, many others, and they don't give a fuck." The blue pill says "life is a struggle and being successful is hard." It doesn't stop there, it goes on to say "if you're not good at something, get better at it. If it’s easy to get, make yourself tough enough to not get it. If you have to work hard, well, it means you are a winner." No surprise there. Red pill tells you "your life is shit. You're a loser. It's gonna be shitty for the next however many years." The blue pill has you assuming that as a lazy poofter you're gonna turn things around, and there’s a big fat zero chance that you’re gonna do that. We all make choices, and red pill makes you confront the shit. 3\) The blue pill betrays who you are. Imagine if a relationship where you had a major personality clash suddenly got better. Blue pill says "well, this person's not a total poofter, but they’re not the greatest either. I guess we're decent. Won't we be able to work together in the future?" Red Pill says "this person betrayed me. If I want to have any chance of a good relationship I have to cut this person out of my life." Blue pill says "well, they're not bad people, but we have to work on the relationship anyway." Not when red pill tells you that the relationship is bad. 4\) It’s a matter of survival. You know what’s easier than telling a kid “well, kids are no good, but if you work hard at it you can get good at this.” It’s having to actually face this, and figure it out. It’s far more terrifying. 5\) Red pill is life-changing. When you face your mortality and the fact that you’re just a sperm in the wind, you tend to look at things with a new outlook. It’s not about stuff like "getting a new car” or “buying that new car”, and watching the world pass you by. It’s about dealing with the moment, and accepting the consequences of it. There’s a lesson to be learned in it, and the truth of it is scarier than any blue pill could ever be. You may think that blue pill is better, but it’s just as much a lie. This red pill shit is real, and it will change your life. Red pill is life-changing. So what’s the deal with this red pill bullshit? I got an email from a German chap, who calls himself Francky, saying “I have read your post on what it means to be a red-pill man. I also got into philosophy when I was in high school and was looking for an answer to what the meaning of life is. When I got into philosophical questions, I came across the red pill and thought to myself, what does this mean?” Francky goes on to tell me about his family and how he struggles with them, but says that the red pill really does make him confront things: “When I’m around my family, it feels like I’m working in a job for them, to keep the family together. Whenever I get upset about that, I read the red-piller’s posts and try to give an explanation, which is something that does help me.” Francky said that he has a few friends who have been taken on to red pill stuff, and he has started reading the same thing they are reading. He said he wants to show them his understanding of things, and get them to see that he sees things differently. I asked Francky what his biggest problem is and I think he summed it up pretty well: “The problem is that I want the red pill to be something that’s related to philosophy. I want it to be clear, but if it’s so black and white, I feel like I don’t understand anything. Sometimes, my family members don’t take anything I say serious, so I try to talk about it with someone. But since most of them only get what the red-piller tells them, I feel like they don’t see that I have thoughts on things, and I feel like they’re too sure of themselves.” I think Francky has it wrong. I think that it’s exactly because of the black and white approach that red pill does make sense. After all, if you’re relying on blue pill philosophy to guide you, you’re not going to get anywhere. As Francky pointed out to me, if you have a problem, the blue pill will tell you the opposite of what</background>
